In _this_ universe, the electrons do not penetrate the glass of the
CRT face. 25 KeV electrons just don't have the range (when electrons
reach the hundreds of KeV range and higher, we call them beta
particles, and even energetic betas are stopped by a few mils of glass
or aluminum).

X-rays are a slightly greater concern, but this was mainly an issue
with early color t.v.s that had too many high-Z things in the beam.
Not a problem today.

Which leaves electromagnetic radiation emitted by monitors, an ongoing
concern (though one I think is full of rumor-mongering scare tactics).

Just setting the record straight.


